Haemophilus influenzae
Infectious Disease & Sexual Health
Acute fulminant high fever/toxicity in unvaccinated child, Epiglottitis (drooling/stridor/tripod position), Bluish-purple facial cellulitis, Gram-negative coccobacilli on chocolate agar

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: IDS-013
Reason for Visit: Acute onset of high fever, stridor, and severe respiratory distress in an unvaccinated child, requiring urgent assessment of airway patency.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and parents
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated an unimmunized child with sudden high fever, profound drooling, and noisy breathing (stridor). Urgent physical assessment is required to rule out acute epiglottitis and impending, catastrophic airway obstruction prior to the next available in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: Parent states: "He woke up burning hot, he can't swallow, he's drooling everywhere, and his breathing is terribly noisy."
History of Present Illness: Illness commenced acutely 6 hours ago with a fulminant onset of high fever and severe systemic toxicity. The parents report a severe, abrupt sore throat and distinctive, continuous drooling as the child refuses to swallow. The child is exhibiting inspiratory stridor and severe dyspnoea, currently assuming the 'tripod' position (leaning forward with hands on knees) to maintain an open airway. The child is unvaccinated due to parental beliefs. No history of foreign body aspiration.
Functional Status: Alert but distressed, tripod positioning in bed.
Social/Safety Concerns: Extremely high acuity situation; parents are highly distressed.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 95/60 | HR: 145 | Temp: 39.8 | RR: 35 | SpO2: 91% | Wt: 18 Kg
General: Toxic, ashen appearance. Sitting forward, mouth open, actively drooling. Audible harsh inspiratory stridor. Minimal agitation (exhausted).
Physical Exam: Respiratory exam reveals severe suprasternal and intercostal retractions with stridor. Child is highly toxic. Crucially, direct visualization of the oral cavity or epiglottis was strictly deferred to prevent precipitating complete acute airway obstruction. There is a distinctive, severe bluish-purple hue on the cheek (early buccal cellulitis). Clinical presentation aligns with the diagnostic tetrad: Acute fulminant high fever/toxicity in unvaccinated child, Epiglottitis (drooling/stridor/tripod position), Bluish-purple facial cellulitis (early signs noted), and Gram-negative coccobacilli on chocolate agar (pending hospital blood cultures).
Environmental Observations: Clear path to the front door for ambulance access.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Haemophilus influenzae type b (Hib) - Acute Epiglottitis - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Viral Croup - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The fulminant toxicity, high fever, severe drooling, and absence of the classic barking cough, which point heavily toward bacterial epiglottitis.

Foreign Body Aspiration - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of high fever, toxic appearance, and bluish facial cellulitis, indicating a severe infectious process.

Severe Tonsillitis / Peritonsillar Abscess - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The rapid onset of stridor and tripod positioning in a highly toxic, unvaccinated child.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Extreme (Impending Airway Catastrophe)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with the classic, fulminant triad of acute epiglottitis: drooling, dysphagia, and distress (stridor) in the setting of an unimmunized status. This is an absolute medical emergency. Any distress, manipulation of the airway, or delay in definitive management poses a critical risk of complete, irreversible airway obstruction and cardiac arrest. An urgent, immediate on-site clinical evaluation was required to confirm the severity, prevent inappropriate interventions by the parents, and coordinate immediate, calm ambulance transfer. This presentation strictly necessitates urgent after-hours intervention (MBS 585-600).

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: IMMEDIATE call to 000 for Priority 1 ambulance transport to an Emergency Department equipped for pediatric airway management. Do not attempt to visualize the throat. Keep the child as calm as possible in the parents' arms. Provide high-flow blow-by oxygen if tolerated without causing agitation.
Positive Behavioral Change: By sitting quietly and holding the child in their preferred tripod position without forcing them to lie down, the parents will minimize airway turbulence; this calm environment will reward the child with optimized air entry, reinforcing the need to keep the child completely undisturbed.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Handover to emergency paramedics and ED staff.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Ambulance is en route. If the child completely stops breathing before paramedics arrive, parents must initiate pediatric basic life support (CPR).

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumed acute epiglottitis; the airway is critically unstable.
Clinical Course: The child requires immediate intubation in a controlled hospital setting (often the operating theatre) and intravenous antibiotics.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not look in his mouth, do not force him to lie down flat, and do not try to give him any food or water. Any distress could cause his airway to snap completely shut.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: A severe bacterial infection has caused the flap over his windpipe to swell massively. We must keep him completely calm and seated forward until the ambulance arrives with specialized breathing equipment.
Actionable Steps: Hold him gently. Keep the room quiet. Do not attempt to look down his throat under any circumstances.

